we use time - evolutions of the linear perturbation equations to study the oscillations of rapidly rotating neutrons stars . In our linear approximation for small rotations, $a$, we solve the problem of an oscillating body, which is described -in the usual coordinate - by a harmonic oscillator; but the approach is applicable to nonlinear oscillations as well. From our solution of perturbating motions, we obtain the amplitude ratios $A_1 / A_2$ of the oscillating stars. We derive an approximation formula for the amplitude ratio of the oscillating neutron stars near the maximum, and compare this result with that by Reisenhart and Zawadowski, which is based on non-oscillatory solutions of the perturbed problem.

We assume that the body may be divided into small parts of masses $m_i$, and that the forces acting on the body in each small part are in weak gradients, i.e.: $$F_{ij} = -  {{\hat F}_{ij}}  
   \label{eq:weq:force}$$ where ${{\hat F}_{ij}}$ is the gradient of the gradient force. Using the assumption in (\[eq:weq:force\]), we can linearize equations describing perturbation of the stellar model by small perturbations (small velocities), where the perturbation operator $\varXi^{(M)} (P)$ has, first, the form of a total multiplication operator. Next, a linearity assumption implies that the total multiplication operator of the linearized equations is equal to the product of independent linear operators, which define the total perturbation of the stellar model. We show that this assumption is correct if the gradients of the perturbation potentials on the body are small. For nonlinear perturbations of small rotations (
